Suppose we have a set of mochi. If we don't use the smallest mochi $A_1$ as a top piece, it might be used as a bottom piece. If $A_1$ is used as a bottom piece, it must be paired with some $a$ such that $a \le A_1/2$. Since $A_1$ is the smallest, $A_1/2$ is smaller than any other $A_i$ (unless $A_i$ are equal). If $A_1$ is the smallest, it's very unlikely to be a top piece for anything unless there's something even smaller, which doesn't exist. So $A_1$ can only be a bottom piece or a top piece.
Actually, if $A_1$ is used as a top piece, it needs a $b \ge 2 A_1$. If $A_1$ is used as a bottom piece, it needs an $a \le A_1/2$. Since $A_1$ is the minimum, $a$ must be $\le A_1/2 < A_1$. This implies $a$ must be strictly smaller than $A_1$ (unless $A_1=0$, but constraints say $A_i \ge 1$). So $A_1$ cannot be a bottom piece unless there is a smaller element, which there isn't. Thus, $A_1$ can only be a top piece.
